(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which organisms colonize the epidermis as normal flora?","choices":["Candida albicans and Aspergillus niger","Coagulase-negative staphylococci and coryneform bacteria","Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes","Escherichia coli and Enterococcus faecalis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — nonpathogenic residents. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does tetracycline cause tooth discoloration?","choices":["It inhibits enamel-forming protein synthesis via the 30S subunit","It causes chronic gingival inflammation","It stains enamel on contact during oral administration","It binds with calcium during tooth development"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why the timing of exposure, during development, is what matters. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which occupation is named as carrying a risk of zoonotic transmission?","choices":["Software engineering","Air traffic control","Veterinary medicine","Accountancy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — with farm work, landscaping, zookeeping and meat processing. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is a zoonosis?","choices":["An infection spread only by insects","An infection acquired only from soil","An animal infection that can spread to humans","An infection that only ever affects animals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Lipopolysaccharide in the Gram-negative outer membrane consists of which three parts?","choices":["Crystal violet, iodine, and safranin","Teichoic acid, lipoteichoic acid, and surface proteins","Lipid A, core polysaccharide, and O-polysaccharide","N-acetyl glucosamine, N-acetyl muramic acid, and a pentaglycine spacer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lipid A carries the endotoxin activity, and the O-polysaccharide is used in bacterial classification. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which sites do nosocomial infections most commonly involve?","choices":["Urinary tract, respiratory tract and surgical incisions","Skin and soft tissue only","Bloodstream and central nervous system infections","Gastrointestinal tract alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Where does bacterial endotoxin sit?","choices":["The flagellum","Lipid A of lipopolysaccharide","A protein secreted by living cells","The bacterial capsule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, released from lysed or damaged gram-negative bacteria. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Where did the idea for anti-fouling surface coatings originate?","choices":["Aircraft manufacture","Textile dyeing","Marine ship coatings","Food packaging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — copper, arsenic, mercury and tin compounds since the 1960s. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which two agents are true chemical sterilants?","choices":["Ethylene oxide, used on plastics and spices, and beta propiolactone, used on vaccines, tissue grafts and surgical instruments","Alcohol and ultraviolet radiation","Chlorine and bromine, used on water and surfaces, and alcohol, used on skin, instruments and laboratory work surfaces","Lysol and Listerine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Very few true chemical sterilants exist. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What complicates eradication when a pathogen can lie dormant in an intermediate host?","choices":["Antibiotics become more effective","Spores and cysts persist","The pathogen mutates into a virus","The animal reservoir dies out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How do factor H and factor I work together?","choices":["Factor H makes C3b susceptible to cleavage by factor I","Factor H cleaves C3b and factor I binds the product","Both increase complement activation","Both form part of the membrane attack complex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a two-step brake. Creating iC3b which cannot form a convertase. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How do biofilms enhance resistance?","choices":["By raising the metabolic rate of the organisms inside them so they divide faster than the drug can act, by physically excluding immune cells from the surface, and by preventing any transfer of resistance genes between the organisms held within","By preventing any gene transfer between organisms","By making the organisms bactericidal to each other","They form a physical and chemical barrier that limits dissemination and sequesters antibiotics; organisms within are metabolically less active and less susceptible; and close spatial relationship enhances resistance gene transfer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three routes are listed together. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"When two drugs are used together, what are the three possible interactions?","choices":["Bactericidal, bacteriostatic and neutral effects","Potentiation, inhibition and tolerance","Indifference — no interaction; synergism — the combined effect is greater than either alone; antagonism — the combined effect is less than either alone","Additive effects, in which the two simply sum; multiplicative effects, in which each amplifies the other; and subtractive effects between the agents"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Using a cocktail is one approach to resistant organisms, but antagonism is the risk that makes the choice matter. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Why do animal viruses require uncoating when phages do not?","choices":["Because the entire virion is engulfed, so the genetic material must then be released — driven by the difference between cytoplasmic and extracellular pH","Because animal viruses have no capsid to remove","Because animal viruses integrate into the host chromosome first, so the genetic material must be excised — driven by the difference in cytoplasmic pH","Because animal viruses replicate outside the cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A phage injects its genome through the wall instead, so nothing needs unwrapping. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"The two wall types are physically versus chemically strong. Which way round?","choices":["Both are described as chemically strong only","Gram-positive is physically strong — resistant to temperature, pH and osmotic pressure; Gram-negative is chemically strong — resistant to disinfectants and antibiotics","Gram-negative is physically strong — resistant to temperature, pH and osmotic pressure; Gram-positive is chemically strong — resistant to disinfectants and antibiotics","Both are described as physically strong only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That difference follows from the thick peptidoglycan sheath versus the outer membrane barrier. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin type is the first receptor on a YOUNG B cell, and which on a mature one?","choices":["A small version of IgM on young cells","IgG on young cells; IgA on mature cells","IgD on young cells; IgM on mature cells","IgE on young cells; IgG on mature cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. IgD on mature cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which structure is a bacterial adhesion mechanism?","choices":["Endospores","Fimbriae","Ribosomes","Plasmids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, also called attachment pili. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is a DAMP, as distinct from a PAMP?","choices":["A pattern found only on bacteria","A receptor on the macrophage surface","A cytokine released by damaged cells","A damage-associated molecular pattern"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: PAMPs mark non-self, DAMPs mark altered self. It signals cells that are damaged, stressed or virus-invaded. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How large is the naive lymphocyte pool, theoretically?","choices":["Up to a quintillion variations","About a thousand variations","About a million variations","Exactly as many as there are antigens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — up to a quintillion. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does incidence measure?","choices":["All existing cases at one moment","Deaths attributable to a disease","The proportion who recover","New cases over a certain time period"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, against the general healthy population. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which proteins prevent soluble C5b, C6 and C7 associating with human membranes?","choices":["CD59 and homologous restriction factor","Decay accelerating factor and membrane cofactor protein","Factor H and factor I","S protein, clusterin and factor J"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What percentage are BASOPHILS, and what is their function?","choices":["20 to 35 per cent; they produce antibody","3 to 7 per cent; they are the largest white cells","0.5 per cent; they release potent chemical mediators","1 to 3 per cent; they destroy eukaryotic pathogens"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — constricted nuclei with dark blue granules. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What distinguishes yeasts from molds?","choices":["Yeasts lack a cell wall entirely; molds have a wall built of chitin","Yeasts are macroscopic; molds are microscopic","Yeasts are photosynthetic; molds are not","Yeasts are unicellular; molds are multicellular and filamentous"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Candida albicans and Saccharomyces cerevisiae are the yeast examples; Penicillium notatum the mold. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What proportion of the human genome is foreign in origin?","choices":["About half","About a tenth","Almost none","About nine tenths"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — viral remnants and transposons. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which patient group is named as being at higher risk from hospital-acquired infection?","choices":["Patients in a waiting room","The immunocompromised","Outpatients attending for blood tests","Healthy adults in primary care"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — with paediatric, geriatric, HIV, burns, open wound and surgical patients. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient develops bacterial pneumonia on top of a viral upper respiratory infection. Which term applies?","choices":["Focal infection","Secondary infection","Superinfection","Mixed infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a different microbe complicating the initial one. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which fragment of an antibody binds the antigen?","choices":["Fc, the crystallisable fragment","The light chain alone","The constant region","Fab, the antigen-binding fragment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — there are two identical ones. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What does the E test (epsilometer test) read, and in what units?","choices":["The therapeutic index, as a ratio","The minimal bactericidal concentration, as a percentage kill","The minimal inhibitory concentration in micrograms per millilitre, at the point where the zone of inhibition meets the strip","The zone diameter in millimetres, corrected for the molecular weight and diffusion rate of the agent tested"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why it is called \"MIC on a stick\". — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"In which form or forms does IgA occur?","choices":["Monomer only","Tetramer only","Monomer or dimer","Pentamer only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, including a free or secretory form. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How many published studies did the keyboard systematic review analyse?","choices":["Seventy-five","Fifteen","Two hundred","Five"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is a superinfection, and what commonly triggers one?","choices":["A relapse of the original infection; triggered by stopping treatment early","A secondary infection acquired often following the use of broad spectrum antibiotics, which reduce normal flora and allow opportunistic infections to take hold","An infection acquired from a medical device; triggered by biofilm formation","An infection with two organisms simultaneously, triggered by mixed exposure during a single episode of care within a healthcare facility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Predisposing conditions include corticosteroid therapy, leukaemia, human immunodeficiency virus, systemic lupus and diabetes. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What did Paul Ehrlich postulate in 1909, and what drug came from that work?","choices":["That drugs could be developed as “magic bullets” targeting infectious organisms without harming the host; arsphenamine (salvarsan), against Treponema pallidum","That moulds inhibit bacterial growth; penicillin","That Streptomyces species produce antibiotics, work which yielded streptomycin and over twenty further agents isolated from common soil organisms","That sulfa dyes could be repurposed as drugs; sulfonamides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That magic-bullet idea is the same principle later formalised as selective toxicity. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Fleming's 1945 warning concerned which specific practice?","choices":["Self-medication with too small doses, so that instead of clearing infection the microbes are “educated” to resist penicillin and penicillin-fast organisms are bred out and passed on","Prescribing penicillin for viral illness","Using antibiotics in animal feed","Combining two antibiotics that act antagonistically, so that the effect of the pair is less than either agent alone would have achieved against the organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He describes the chain ending in someone who develops a septicaemia or pneumonia that penicillin cannot save. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What causes the stationary phase?","choices":["Intensifying limiting factors killing cells exponentially","Depleted nutrients and oxygen, plus excretion of organic acids and pollutants, so growth rate equals death rate","Cells adjusting and enlarging before division begins","Adequate nutrients and favourable environment, plus removal of organic acids and pollutants, so growth continues unchecked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The balance point between division and death is what makes the curve flatten. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What two conditions must chemical disinfectants meet to work?","choices":["A warm room and bright light","Application by a doctor","Use within one hour of opening","Concentration and contact time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Griseofulvin and tolnaftate act by which mechanisms respectively?","choices":["Both interfere with nucleic acid synthesis","Both act only systemically, given intravenously","Griseofulvin inhibits microtubule formation during cell division, interfering with mitosis; tolnaftate inhibits squalene epoxidase and therefore ergosterol synthesis","Griseofulvin binds ergosterol within the fungal membrane and forms pores; tolnaftate blocks the synthesis of beta-glucan in the fungal cell wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Griseofulvin is used for superficial dermatophytes; tolnaftate distorts fungal hyphae and stunts mycelial growth. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Dysregulation of which interleukin is linked to lupus and rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":["Interleukin 1","Interleukin 6","Interleukin 12","Interleukin 2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What three things must the recognition system distinguish between?","choices":["Antigen, antibody and complement","Healthy self, non-self, and ALTERED self","Bacteria, viruses and fungi","Extracellular, intracellular and surface pathogens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — altered self covers virus-infected and cancerous cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which consequence of hospital-acquired infection is named?","choices":["Longer hospital stays and preventable deaths","Shorter admissions from earlier discharge","Reduced treatment cost","Improved quality of care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — with long-term disability and expense. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What can defensins kill?","choices":["Only intracellular parasites","Bacteria, fungi","Bacteria only","Non-enveloped viruses only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the envelope is a membrane, which is what they damage. ENVELOPED viruses. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is a fomite defined?","choices":["A non-living surface or object","A living organism that carries disease to a host","A person who is infected but has no symptoms","The route a pathogen takes to leave the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which T cell destroys foreign or abnormal cells by secreting lytic enzymes?","choices":["The delayed hypersensitivity cell, TD","The T suppressor cell","The cytotoxic T cell, CD8","The T helper cell, CD4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it especially targets virally infected cells, cancer cells, and cells from other humans and animals. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is a reservoir of infection?","choices":["The population currently infected","The minimum dose needed to infect","The primary habitat a pathogen originates from","The route by which a pathogen exits a host"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; it may be living or nonliving. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does the ESKAPE group refer to, and why does it matter clinically?","choices":["Clinically relevant biofilm producers — Enterococcus faecium, Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Acinetobacter baumannii, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Enterobacter species — which colonise medical devices and host tissues, promoting persistent infection","The six antibiotic classes most often rendered useless by beta-lactamase enzymes — penicillins, cephalosporins, monobactams, carbapenems, glycopeptides and macrolides — which together account for most treatment failure in hospital-acquired infection worldwide","A monitoring programme for resistance in enteric bacteria","The six genetic mechanisms by which resistance develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The devices named are urinary catheters, ventilators, prosthetic joints and cardiac implants; the tissues are chronic wounds and the lungs of cystic fibrosis patients. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which receptor is found on T HELPER cells, and which on T CYTOTOXIC cells?","choices":["CD4 on both","CD8 on both","CD4 on helper, CD8 on cytotoxic","CD8 on helper, CD4 on cytotoxic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Hypersensitivity reactions are most often seen with which drugs, and what range do they cover?","choices":["Tetracyclines and aminoglycosides; reactions may be immediate or delayed, ranging from photosensitivity through to permanent tooth discoloration","Beta lactam antibiotics such as penicillin and cephalosporins, and also quinolones; reactions may be immediate or delayed, ranging from hives to anaphylactic shock","Aminoglycosides; ranging from tinnitus to deafness","Sulfonamides only; limited to rash"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both the drug families and the immediate-versus-delayed distinction are given. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What directs B-cell maturation?","choices":["The thymus gland","The spleen","Gut-associated lymphoid tissue","Bone marrow stromal cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the stromal cells nurture the lymphocyte stem cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which patient group has weakened defences and is therefore at risk?","choices":["The elderly, young children and premature infants","Healthy young adults","Athletes","Those whose resident flora remain fully intact"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside those on immunosuppressants. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is distinctive about how MEMORY cells are formed?","choices":["They fuse with plasma cells","They PAUSE partway through mitosis","They complete mitosis and then rest","They never divide at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is why the secondary response is so much faster. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which is the first of Koch's classic postulates?","choices":["Cultivate the microbe in the laboratory","Inoculate a healthy susceptible subject","Re-isolate the agent from the new host","Find the microbe in every case of the disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What does the ending -mab signify in a drug name?","choices":["A humanised antibody specifically","A mouse-derived antibody","A chimeric antibody","A monoclonal antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and these are proteins. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"How does Salmonella enterica establish a niche for replication in gut cells?","choices":["By producing a capsule that prevents phagocytosis","By binding the sialic acid receptors on host cells with a surface hemagglutinin protein","By producing an endotoxin released only on cell lysis","By using a secretion system that injects effector proteins into host gut cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That secretion system is what allows entry into cells. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Roughly what share of human infections do viruses cause, according to the Boone and Gerba review?","choices":["About twenty-five per cent","About sixty per cent","About ten per cent","About ninety per cent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which three cytokines have systemic effects including FEVER?","choices":["Interferons alpha, beta and gamma","CXCL8, CCL2 and interleukin 12","C3a, C5a and properdin","Interleukin 1 beta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same three that act locally. Interleukin 6 and tumour necrosis factor alpha. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A Gram-positive cell wall consists of which features?","choices":["An asymmetric outer bilayer of lipopolysaccharide over a periplasmic space and a thin shell of peptidoglycan, losing crystal violet and staining red","No cell wall at all, giving a pleomorphic shape","A carbohydrate-based wall with 80S ribosomes","A thick homogenous sheath of peptidoglycan 20-80 nm thick, with teichoic and lipoteichoic acid, retaining crystal violet and staining purple"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is described as physically strong — resistant to stresses such as temperature, pH and osmotic pressure. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the term sequelae mean?","choices":["The route by which a pathogen leaves","A second infection complicating the first","Long-term or permanent tissue or organ damage","The period before symptoms appear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — paralysis from polio, deafness from meningitis. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which cytokine drives the acute-phase response, and which cells release it?","choices":["Tumour necrosis factor alpha, released by mast cells","Interleukin 6, released by macrophages","Interferon gamma, released by T cells","Interleukin 1 beta, released by neutrophils"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — bacteria induce macrophages to make it, and it acts on liver cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How do phagocytes limit damage from toxic oxygen species?","choices":["They stop producing them after one minute","They rely on complement to neutralise them","They synthesise enzymes such as catalase","They expel the species into the bloodstream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — toxic species can otherwise diffuse out and harm host cells. Which degrades hydrogen peroxide to water and oxygen. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"During which phase are bacteria most vulnerable to control methods, and what is an example?","choices":["Lag phase; example, refrigeration","Stationary phase; example, autoclaving","Exponential growth phase; example, penicillin","Death phase; example, ultraviolet radiation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin acts on wall synthesis, so it bites hardest while cells are actively dividing. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which three differences separate animal virus from phage replication?","choices":["The entire virion is engulfed, uncoating is required to release the genetic material, and release is by budding — which may assist acquisition of an envelope","The entire virion is engulfed, no uncoating is required at any point, and release occurs only by lysis of the host cell membrane rather than by budding","Animal viruses lack a capsid, replicate in the nucleus, and never integrate","Animal viruses bind only protein receptors, use no enzymes, and kill every host cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Uncoating is driven by the difference between cytoplasmic and extracellular pH. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 58"}]};
